            "The Carnegie Endowment promoted an international order grounded in law, arbitration and informed public discussion. Its Division of Intercourse and Education circulated publications on treaties and international affairs, supported libraries and arranged exchanges among scholars, jurists and journalists. A 1929 tour brought European journalists to the United States to meet American professional counterparts, after which invitations moved in the other direction. Such programmes were intended to widen acquaintance and build networks able to interpret national positions across borders. They also selected which texts, professions and institutions deserved support. The Endowment’s peace objective therefore worked through concrete gatekeeping decisions, and its private standing did not make those decisions politically neutral.",
            "The Rockefeller Foundation followed a different substantive route, concentrating heavily on natural and medical science, public health and the institutions that produced expert knowledge. Grants and fellowships supported scientists, physicians, health boards and educational organisations; cooperation with League of Nations bodies enabled professional exchange; journals and equipment helped reconnect European institutions weakened by war. The expected mechanism joined expertise to relationship-building: shared research and professional standards could create practical cooperation and a more stable international order. Yet scientific authority carried power. Foundation officers defined eligible fields and assessed need, while well-resourced American institutions often set standards. Knowledge exchange could be genuinely collaborative and still reproduce unequal control over priorities and resources."
