            "The same matrix supports causal analysis. Access to a respected institution may create an opportunity for learning, while an expert network may circulate knowledge and provide trusted contacts during crisis. Neither mechanism guarantees influence. Participants can reject advice; professional communities can remain socially narrow; and expertise can be used for purposes the original sponsor did not intend. Analysts should specify the intermediate result and seek evidence at each step. A list of grants demonstrates allocation, correspondence may reveal exchange, and institutional records may show application. Policy change requires an additional link and comparison with domestic political, economic and strategic explanations.",
            "A defensible judgement holds fragmentation’s costs and benefits together. The interwar field lacked unified strategy and accountability, but its diversity created resilience, specialist knowledge and relationships that central government could not command into existence. Private status sometimes supported credibility, though it could also hide unaccountable power. State involvement sometimes supplied continuity, though it could politicise exchange. The relevant balance varied by country, audience and purpose; fragmentation was a condition to analyse, not a verdict in itself. Schindler’s larger contribution is to make these hybrid arrangements visible as part of American statecraft without flattening them into a single apparatus. The period matters on its own terms and as a reservoir of people, practices and debates that shaped the more crowded institutional landscape of the Second World War."
